      "sequence_position": 8,
      "problem": "Find, in ascending powers of $y$ and descending powers of $z,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion of ${(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}}-{z}^{2})}^{3}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=3,$ $a=\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}},$ and $b=-{z}^{2}.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,3,3,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}}-{z}^{2})}^{3} & =1{a}^{3}+3{a}^{2}b+3a{b}^{2}+1{b}^{3} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}})}^{3}{(-{z}^{2})}^{0}+3{(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}})}^{2}{(-{z}^{2})}^{1} \\\\  & \\,+3{(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}})}^{1}{(-{z}^{2})}^{2}+1{(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}})}^{0}{(-{z}^{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =1(\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{{z}^{6}})(1)+3(\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{{z}^{4}})(-{z}^{2}) \\\\  & \\,+3(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}})({z}^{4})+1(1)(-{z}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{{z}^{6}}-\\frac{12{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}{{z}^{4}}+\\frac{6y{z}^{4}}{{z}^{2}}-{z}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{{z}^{6}}-\\frac{12{y}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}+6y{z}^{2}-{z}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the binomial expansion in ascending powers of $y$ and descending powers of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}}-{z}^{2})}^{3}=-{z}^{6}+6y{z}^{2}-\\frac{12{y}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}+\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{{z}^{6}}\n$$\n\nFinally, in ascending powers of $y$ and descending powers of $z,$ the first three terms are\n\n$$\n-{z}^{6}+6y{z}^{2}-\\frac{12{y}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}.\n$$",
